'''blamer''' (blā′mėr), ''n.'' One who blames, finds fault, or censures: as, “''blamers'' of the times,” ''Donne'', To Countess of Bedford, iii.

'''blameworthiness''' (blām′wėr″ᴛ̵ʜi-nes), ''n.'' [< ''blameworthy'' + ''-ness.''] The quality of being blameworthy; blamableness.
{{smaller block|Praise and blame express what actually are, praiseworthiness and ''blameworthiness'' what naturally ought to be, the sentiments of other people with regard to our character and conduct.
{{right|''Adam Smith'', Theory of Moral Sentiments, iii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|Blame I can bear, though not ''blameworthiness.''
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, I. 140.}}}}

'''blameworthy''' (blām′wėr″ᴛ̵ʜi), ''a.'' [< ME. ''blameworthy'', < ''blame'' + ''worthy.''] Deserving blame; censurable; culpable; reprehensible.
{{smaller block|That the sending of a divorce to her husband was not ''blameworthy'', he affirms, because the man was heinously vicious.
{{right|''Milton'', Divorce, ii. 22.}}}}

