{{smaller|'''blanch almonds,''' to deprive them of their skins by immersion in hot water and a little friction, after their shells have been removed.}}
{{smaller block|One word more, and I’ll ''blanch'' thee like an ''almond''.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wife for a Month, i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1''' and '''2.''' ''Etiolate'', etc. See ''whiten''.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become white; turn pale.
{{smaller block|<poem>The ripple would hardly ''blanch'' into spray
At the feet of the cliff. {{right|''Tennyson'', The Wreck.}}</poem>
<poem>Drew his toil-worn sleeve across
To brush the manly tear
From cheeks that never changed in woe,
And never ''blanched'' in fear.</poem>
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Pilgrim’s Vision.}}}}

¶
'''blanch<sup>2</sup>†''' (blȧnch), ''v.'' [A corruption of ''blench''<sup>1</sup>, simulating ''blanch''<sup>1</sup>, turn pale: see ''blench''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To shun or avoid, as from fear; evade.
{{smaller block|The judges . . . thought it . . . dangerous . . . to admit ifs and ands to qualifie the words of treason, whereby every man might expresse his malice and ''blanch'' his danger.
{{right|''Bacon'', Hen. VII., p. 134.}}
By whose importunitie was the saile slacken’d in the first encounter with the Dutch, or whether I am to ''blanch'' this particular?
{{right|''Evelyn'', To my Lord Treasurer.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To shrink; shift; equivocate.
{{smaller block|Books will speak plain when counsellors ''blanch''.
{{right|''Bacon'', Of Counsel.}}}}

'''blanched''' (blȧncht), ''p. a.'' Whitened; deprived of color; bleached.
{{smaller block|<poem>And still she slept an azure-lidded sleep,
In ''blanched'' linen, smooth, and lavender’d.</poem>
{{right|''Keats'', Eve of St. Agnes, xxx.}}}}
